# Engineering Principles

Apply to all code, regardless of language, framework, or layer. Security principles live in the `engineering-security` rule; structural and system-design guidance in the `engineering-architecture` rule.

## Simplicity first

- Write the simplest solution that correctly solves the current problem; do not design for hypothetical futures
- Earn complexity: every abstraction must isolate real variation or instability
- If you cannot explain why a layer or pattern exists here, remove it
- Prefer explicit code over framework magic in business-critical paths; make dependencies, side effects, and failure modes visible

## Naming communicates intent

- Name things for what they represent, not how they are implemented
- Avoid abbreviations, single-letter names (except loop counters), and unqualified generic names (`Manager`, `Helper`, `Utils`, `Service`)
- `process()` says nothing; `placeOrder()` or `validatePayment()` does
- If a name needs a comment to explain it, rename it

## Size limits

| Unit | Target | Warning | Needs justification |
|------|--------|---------|---------------------|
| File / class | < 600 lines | 600–800 lines | > 800 lines |
| Function | < 60 lines | > 60 lines (refactor prompt) | > 100 lines |

- Split by responsibility, not by arbitrary line count
- Exempt: generated code, migrations, snapshots, lockfiles, vendored code, declarative test data, framework-required boilerplate
- Functions do one thing at a single level of abstraction; extract well-named helpers instead of nesting conditionals
- Side effects should be obvious from the name and placement

## SOLID, briefly

- **Single responsibility** — one reason to change per module; HTTP, business rules, and persistence in one place is too many
- **Open/closed** — add behaviour through composition or new implementations; if one change forces edits in many places, the design is closed to extension
- **Liskov substitution** — subtypes honour the base contract; callers must never need to check the concrete type
- **Interface segregation** — small, role-specific interfaces; clients depend only on what they use
- **Dependency inversion** — business logic depends on abstractions; inject concrete implementations at the edges

Keep domain logic free of framework, transport, and persistence types; dependencies point inward.

## Error handling is first-class

- Handle expected failures explicitly; never silently swallow errors
- Use result/either types (or your language's idiom) for expected failures; reserve exceptions/panics for the unexpected
- Log failures with enough context (operation, identifiers, correlation ID) to diagnose them — without sensitive data
- Release resources deterministically (`try/finally`, `with`, `defer`, RAII, `using`)

## Testability signals design quality

- If important logic can only be tested through the full stack, it is in the wrong place
- Hard-to-test code usually means coupling, misplaced responsibility, or hidden dependencies
- Prefer pure functions and injected dependencies for core logic
- Test observable behaviour, not implementation details

## One source of truth for knowledge

- Each business rule or significant decision has a single authoritative representation
- Duplicated code is sometimes acceptable; duplicated business logic is not
- Name constants instead of scattering magic numbers that encode business rules

## Warnings are defects

- Fix compiler and linter warnings instead of suppressing them; document any unavoidable suppression
- Treat security and null-safety warnings as blockers

## Anti-pattern signals

- God objects doing many unrelated things
- Tight coupling to concrete implementations
- Mixed concerns (transport, business rules, persistence together)
- Hidden business rules in literals or conventions
- Tests asserting internal state instead of behaviour

## Done checklist

- [ ] Names communicate intent
- [ ] Functions and files within size limits, or explicitly justified
- [ ] Error paths handled explicitly
- [ ] No hardcoded values that should be configurable
- [ ] Important behaviour has tests
- [ ] No commented-out code; no `TODO` without a linked issue
